{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_evqzpe6ngjcem5u6ib7xxmsn6ndyzaamnpfhbzk7jt2scooz7hiq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **All four phases pass; 2 real divergences were found and fixed in the Rust.**\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md`: 0 missing/extra symbols, 0 undefined non-libc | ✅ C exports `next_double`; Rust exports `next_double` |\n| Phase B: every `CONFIGS.md` row passes on randomized inputs | ✅ 30/30 rows, ~1.1 M calls per library |\n| Binary stdout comparison | N/A — no `add_executable`/`[[bin]]`; asserted by `cfg_29`, not assumed |\n| Phase C: every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing test | ✅ 10/10 rows |\n| Holds under every feature combination | ✅ 43 tests × 3 feature sets × 2 profiles (no `[features]` table exists) |\n\n## Divergences found and fixed\n\n**D1 — misaligned pointer aborted instead of returning a value.** `next_double` did `let rnd = unsafe { &mut *rnd };`. Forming a reference from a misaligned raw pointer trips Rust's alignment check → `SIGABRT`, while C returns a `double`. Fixed by taking `*mut cn_rnd_t` and using `addr_of_mut!` + `read_unaligned`/`write_unaligned` — never forming a reference. This is a genuine code fix: it passes even with `RUSTFLAGS=\"-C debug-assertions=on\"`.\n\n**D2 — `next_double(NULL)` died with the wrong signal.** C: `SIGSEGV (11)`. Rust: `SIGABRT (6)`, because Rust's null precondition check fires before the load can fault. Both \"failed\", but differently — exactly what Phase C exists to catch. Fixed with `[profile.dev] debug-assertions/overflow-checks = false` (rationale documented inline), so the debug `.so` carries the same instrumentation as the release one. Residual documented in `ERRORS.md`: a forced `-C debug-assertions=on` override re-introduces it.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n- **`cargo test` does not rebuild a `cdylib`.** A stale `.so` was initially being verified, which masked D1 entirely. Now guarded three ways: a staleness test, profile-matched `.so` selection derived from `current_exe()` (not mtime, and not `cfg!(debug_assertions)` — which the new profile settings would make lie), and `run_verification.sh` always building before testing.\n- **The harness was mutation-tested.** 9 mutants injected into `src/lib.rs` (shift constants, exponent, `wrapping_add`, the `- 1.0`, state-write order) were each built into a separate `.so` and loaded in place of the real one — all 9 caught, 31–36 failing tests each. This matters because my first mutation attempt silently patched only the *doc-comment* copy of the C code and appeared to show the suite was insensitive.\n\nOne correction I made to my own artifacts, not the code: I initially wrote the max output as `1.0 - 2^-53`; C and Rust agreed with each other and both gave `1.0 - 2^-52` (`0x3FEFFFFFFFFFFFFE`), which is correct since the mantissa step in `[1,2)` is 2⁻⁵². `c_src/` is unmodified (only `build/` added).\n\nReproduce with `bash translation/run_verification.sh`. Note it defaults to `CARGO_FLAGS=--offline` since crates.io was unreachable here; `libloading 0.8.9` came from the local cargo cache."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1000,"cache_read_input_tokens":118656,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1000,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31613e31-216e-43f5-84fd-b1ffa262aed0","uuid":"820bd45e-27fa-47b5-b879-ec21195c41b7","timestamp":"2026-09-05T01:33:23.909Z"}
